Wood weathering, decay and biological exposure (@mat-wood-degradation: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @materials-engineering, #source "../sources/Wood-Handbook-FPL-2021.lingua#r_2SP2KMHCD78MQGZN69GKPGJ3WM", #materials-engineering "Interfaces-deposits-and-combined-exposure.lingua#r_28CQ1R0G083JT921V58ECCVFHR") { r_16JPZZQQVC7Q87Q4418D4GEZ4F
**Read:** [Wood Handbook — Forest Products Laboratory, 2021](../sources/Wood-Handbook-FPL-2021.lingua#r_2SP2KMHCD78MQGZN69GKPGJ3WM), chapters 14–16 in full, with chapters 4 and 13 for moisture and drying. Chapter 14 is by Rachel Arango, Stan T. Lebow and Jessie A. Glaeser.

Separate surface weathering, dimensional response, fungal stain, structural decay, insect damage and marine-borer attack. They involve different agents and exposure conditions. Moisture history and detailing affect whether favorable conditions persist; species, sapwood/heartwood, prior treatment and access paths matter. Appearance alone does not quantify retained load capacity.

**Work:** Reconstruct a service case with wood identity, moisture/temperature history, geometry and evidence for the damaging agent. Compare surface and internal observations and identify the uncertainty in remaining useful section or properties. Explain a prevention strategy through its mechanism and limits. Read preservation methods as material-science evidence; any operational choice of a regulated treatment requires the applicable current product and use information.

**Return:** [Materials engineering — structure, processing and useful behavior](../Materials-engineering.lingua#r_05BF5JM8141RKF1VT5998YFZQ9).
**Continue in materials engineering:** [Interfaces, deposits and combined exposure](Interfaces-deposits-and-combined-exposure.lingua#r_28CQ1R0G083JT921V58ECCVFHR).

